igible': False, 'edits_remaining': '5'}, 'is_translatable': False, 'views': {'count': '303', 'state': 'EnabledWithCount'}, 'source': '<a href="http://twitter.com/download/iphone" rel="nofollow">Twitter for iPhone</a>', 'note_tweet': {'is_expandable': True, 'note_tweet_results': {'result': {'id': 'Tm90ZVR3ZWV0OjE4ODUxNTUzMTc5NzUwNTYzODQ=', 'text': 'And yet here are examples of “settles science” being refuted and it was the right thing! \n\nThere have been several cases where scientific understanding of vaccines has evolved, overturning previously accepted beliefs. Here are a couple of notable examples:\n\n1. The Cutter Incident (1955) – Polio Vaccine Safety\n\nPast Settled Science:\n\nWhen Jonas Salk’s inactivated polio vaccine (IPV) was introduced in 1955, it was believed that the manufacturing process effectively killed all live poliovirus, making the vaccine completely safe.\n\nScientific Overturning:\n\nThat same year, a batch of vaccines from Cutter Laboratories contained live poliovirus due to a manufacturing error, causing 40,000 cases of polio, 200 cases of paralysis, and 10 deaths. This led to a deeper understanding of vaccine production safety and the eventual shift to Albert Sabin’s oral polio vaccine (OPV), which used a weakened but live virus that was safer in mass immunization. Later, due to concerns over rare cases of vaccine-derived polio, some countries switched back to IPV.\n\n2. The Change in Whooping Cough (Pertussis) Vaccine – Whole-Cell vs. Acellular\n\nPast Settled Science:\n\nThe whole-cell pertussis vaccine (DTP) was introduced in the 1940s and was believed to be highly effective at preventing whooping cough. It worked, but it also caused high fevers, swelling, and, in rare cases, severe neurological reactions in some children.\n\nScientific Overturning:\n\nBy the 1990s, research showed that while the vaccine was effective, the side effects were a concern. Scientists developed the acellular pertussis vaccine (DTaP), which had fewer side effects but later was found to provide shorter-lasting immunity than the whole-cell version. Some researchers now debate whether a return to whole-cell pertussis vaccines (or a modified version) may be necessary due to rising cases of pertussis.\n\nPer Chat GPT', 'entity_set': {'hashtags': [], 'symbols': [], 'urls': [], 'user_mentions': []}}}}, 'legacy': {'bookmark_count': 0, 'bookmarked': False, 'created_at': 'Fri Jan 31 02:37:06 +0000 2025', 'conversation_id_str': '1885020473618055460', 'display_text_range': [26, 306], 'entities': {'hashtags': [], 'symbols': [], 'timestamps': [], 'urls': [], 'user_mentions': [{'id_str': '69156861', 'name': 'Chief Nerd', 'screen_name': 'TheChiefNerd', 'indices': [0, 13]}, {'id_str': '289420613', 'name': 'Dr. Naomi Wolf. 8 NYT Bestsellers. DPhil, Poetry.', 'screen_name': 'naomirwolf', 'indices': [14, 25]}]}, 'favorite_count': 0, 'favorited': False, 'full_text': '@TheChiefNerd @naomirwolf And yet here are examples of “settles science” being refuted and it was the right thing! \n\nThere have been several cases where scientific understanding of vaccines has evolved, overturning previously accepted beliefs. Here are a couple of notable examples:\n\n1. The Cutter Incident', 'in_reply_to_screen_name': 'TheChiefNerd', 'in_reply_to_status_id_str': '1885020473618055460', 'in_reply_to_user_id_str': '69156861', 'is_quote_status': False, 'lang': 'en', 'quote_count': 0, 'reply_count': 0, 'retweet_count': 0, 'retweeted': False, 'user_id_str': '212776326', 'id_str': '1885155318146982191'}}, '_legacy': {'bookmark_count': 0, 'bookmarked': False, 'created_at': 'Fri Jan 31 02:37:06 +0000 2025', 'conversation_id_str': '1885020473618055460', 'display_text_range': [26, 306], 'entities': {'hashtags': [], 'symbols': [], 'timestamps': [], 'urls': [], 'user_mentions': [{'id_str': '69156861', 'name': 'Chief Nerd', 'screen_name': 'TheChiefNerd', 'indices': [0, 13]}, {'id_str': '289420613', 'name': 'Dr. Naomi Wolf. 8 NYT Bestsellers. DPhil, Poetry.', 'screen_name': 